WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.510
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
and today I want to talk about

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.380
listening comprehension, how to
improve our listening comprehension,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.540
uh, based on my experience.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.990
Uh, remember that that listening
comprehension, at least for me

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.379
is, is the fundamental goal,
the fundamental activity.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.280
You wanna understand
what people are saying.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:22.740
If you can understand movies, if you
can understand podcasts, if you can

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.030
understand, you know, conversations,
the other end of the conversation, you

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.810
can eventually get to where you can
hold up your end of the conversation.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.750
Uh, I spend up most of my time listening.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.680
Listening comprehension is, is
where the action is in my opinion.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:38.280
Of course.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.930
I mentioned reading as a way to increase
your vocabulary so that later on

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.960
when you hear these words, you might
have a chance to understand them.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.930
But listening comprehension is
where I spend most of my time.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.950
But before I get into more detail,
I want to tell you two stories.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.655
So first of all, Uh, the
first takes place in St.

00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:57.705
Petersburg, Russia.

00:00:58.095 --> 00:01:02.055
I had been studying Russian for
four years, I guess, off and on.

00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:05.054
I listened to audio
books, Russian literature.

00:01:05.054 --> 00:01:08.625
I listened to political
discussions from a radio station.

00:01:08.955 --> 00:01:12.225
I understood a lot of
Russian, and I'm in St.

00:01:12.225 --> 00:01:12.795
Petersburg.

00:01:12.825 --> 00:01:18.315
I want to buy a train ticket to go to
Vyborg, which is just north of, of St.

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:22.145
Petersburg on the electric train.

00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:28.485
And so I'm waiting in line and, uh,
finally it's my turn to tell the

00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:32.445
lady there behind the counter what
I wanted and, uh, a certain amount

00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:36.645
of trepidation here, you know, using
the language in not to listen to

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:40.725
political discussion, but to use it in
a very practical, specific situation.

00:01:41.965 --> 00:01:44.570
And I said, I would like to
buy a ticket to go to Vyborg.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.390
And she comes back at me with
something and I didn't understand

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.650
what she said and I had to repeat
and, and ask her to repeat.

00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:55.020
And finally, whatever she
said, I can't remember.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.320
Uh, I got the picture and I
paid her and I got my ticket.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.060
So that's one situation.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.130
Uh, another situation I was
talking to, we have a Mexican

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.090
gardener here in Palm Springs.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.634
And, uh, we were talking about
different things and all of a

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:14.234
sudden he said, uh, I said, what?

00:02:14.415 --> 00:02:15.975
"Pow", "pow", what's "pow"?

00:02:16.725 --> 00:02:19.935
And finally, I figured out it was pago.

00:02:19.935 --> 00:02:21.225
It had to do with his payment.

00:02:21.225 --> 00:02:23.985
Somehow his bank transfers
weren't going through.

00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:27.704
What's the significance of
these two little stories?

00:02:28.635 --> 00:02:33.175
I have found time and time again
that in order to understand a part.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:40.049
Um, context, you kind of have
to have experienced it before.

00:02:40.529 --> 00:02:44.970
So you have to be able to
anticipate what is coming at you.

00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:49.290
And if you have not had that
particular situation before,

00:02:49.529 --> 00:02:51.269
it will be more difficult.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:56.790
So the second time, or the third
time that I go to buy a train ticket

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.600
in Russia, it'll be easier for me
'cause I've already experienced it.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.910
I can anticipate what the
person's going to say.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:09.299
Uh, similarly with, with this Mexican
gardener, if I knew kind of roughly the

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:13.079
context of what he was talking about,
I might have been able to connect

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:17.070
the fact that Pago to him was almost
"pow", or at least I heard it as "pow".

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.609
And this is important because
there's so many different accents,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.859
even within a particular accent.

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:25.019
People have different ways
of pronouncing things.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.820
It's very important to
therefore be able to anticipate.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.400
And what that means is when you don't
understand, don't criticize yourself.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.750
Don't blame yourself.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.890
The fact that you're in a situation
where you don't understand is a good

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:46.080
thing because you have now time to
think about that, and so next time

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.990
around you probably will do better.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.700
I mean, you can do things just by
dent of doing a lot of listening.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.810
You are exposing yourself to
more different situations.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.380
Even if you're, you know, watching a
series on Netflix, uh, you're sort of

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.960
involved now in a, in an environment.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.560
It might be a family, it might be,
gosh knows whatever is happening

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.370
and as you spend more and more time
in that situation, you're gradually

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.940
going to understand more because
you're becoming familiar with, with

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.840
what people say to each other within
that particular context that you

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.435
have been sort of catapulted into.

00:04:24.765 --> 00:04:29.415
So I, I only wanna make this point because
I, I think people sometimes get very

00:04:29.415 --> 00:04:33.675
upset if, if they feel they have a good
level of comprehension and then they're

00:04:33.675 --> 00:04:38.895
put in a very specific situation and they
have trouble understanding what is said.

00:04:39.225 --> 00:04:40.305
No harm done.

00:04:40.335 --> 00:04:44.655
You ask the person to repeat as
I did with a Mexican gardener or

00:04:44.655 --> 00:04:46.125
with a, I shouldn't say Mexican.

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:49.745
He's a, he's an American, but he doesn't
speak English here in Palm Springs.

00:04:50.185 --> 00:04:53.775
Uh, or with the, uh, the lady
selling the train ticket in St.

00:04:53.775 --> 00:04:55.845
Petersburg, I just ask again.

00:04:56.385 --> 00:05:00.765
And, uh, so, you know, my advice,
uh, uh, in terms of listening

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:05.445
comprehension is, you know, expose
yourself to as many different accents.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.990
As many different situations, as
many different contexts as possible.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.010
And recognize that there will always
be situations where even some of the

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:18.510
most basic bits of vocabulary will
pass you by things that you think

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:19.920
you should be able to understand.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.480
You won't understand them.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.340
And that's just part of improving
your listening comprehension.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:31.510
And uh, and that's why I should say
by the way, that exposing yourself

00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:36.375
to, you know, speaking situations,
not being afraid to make mistakes,

00:05:36.914 --> 00:05:39.255
not being afraid to not understand.

00:05:39.675 --> 00:05:43.365
Having one situation where you didn't
understand and you go back in there again.

00:05:43.425 --> 00:05:46.905
And the third time you will
understand that particular context.

00:05:46.935 --> 00:05:51.735
And there are so many other contexts that
you need to, you know, expose yourself to.

00:05:52.094 --> 00:05:58.020
Uh, so a big part of language, you know,
in progressing in our languages is having

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.030
a sense of confidence, and that means
not becoming overly concerned about

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.159
situations where you didn't understand.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.599
Just keep going.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.010
So I have talked before about listening
comprehension, and I'll leave you with

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.359
a couple of videos on the subject.

00:06:12.359 --> 00:06:13.699
I hope this is helpful.

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:14.669
Bye for now.

